sg-session (0.1.0-148) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every session has a keyring (Secret Service, org.freedesktop.secrets),
    opened by the sign-in password: sg-session depends on gnome-keyring,
    libpam-gnome-keyring and libsecret-tools. Eddie said "Unable to talk
    with OS keyring to save the password" and saved it in plain text;
    Chromium, Chrome, VS Code, Element, Thunderbird and NetworkManager
    applets expect one too. Signing in opens it through Debian's own greetd
    PAM file (pam_gnome_keyring was there, the module was not installed);
    installed machines get it with apt upgrade.
  * The lock screen reopens a closed keyring with the password typed:
    stained-glass-lock runs pam_gnome_keyring after the password check, and
    sg-rdp-pamcheck (console path) tells it the person's runtime directory,
    which sg-lockd, a system service, does not have.
  * sg-password-change (new, root; sg-admind's user-password-own): a person
    changing their own password, with the current one, as passwd(1) would
    run for them (PAM stained-glass-password = common-password): pam_unix
    checks the current password and the rules, pam_gnome_keyring
    re-encrypts the keyring, so it still opens at the next sign-in --
    signed in (the session's daemon) or signed out (a private daemon for the
    change). An administrator's reset still cannot (no current password).
  * sg-keyring-first (new; pam_exec in the auth stack, pam-configs
    stained-glass-keyring, greetd only): makes the login keyring at a
    person's first sign-in, before the session's daemon opens it.
    gnome-keyring does not publish on D-Bus a keyring it creates through
    its control socket, so the first program to store a secret got a "new
    keyring password" prompt instead (found by the VM gate).
  * test/keyring-test.sh (make test-keyring, test-keyring-mutants): the
    first sign-in in a session-like bus, then secret-tool stores and finds
    a secret with no prompt; the lock screen's check reopens a locked keyring, a wrong
    password does not; as root, sg-password-change refuses a wrong current
    password and re-encrypts the keyring signed out and in. Mutants
    LOCK_KEYRING, PWCHANGE_RUID, PWCHANGE_RUNTIME, KEYRING_FIRST each fail
    it.

