sg-session (0.1.0-121) unstable; urgency=medium

  * /boot on the root file system (all installed machines): /boot was the
    FAT boot partition, where dpkg cannot upgrade a kernel package in place
    ("unable to make backup link": no hard links on FAT) -- a Debian
    revision of the same kernel would have left the update stuck.
    sg-boot-layout: Debian's kernel files live in the root file system's
    /boot; the ESP is mounted at /efi and our own boot partition (beside
    another system's ESP) at /xbootldr, from /etc/fstab (automounts,
    nofail); kernel-install copies kernels there (install.conf BOOT_ROOT=),
    services get SYSTEMD_XBOOTLDR_PATH. sg-install sets it up on every new
    system; sg-boot-layout.service moves an installed machine over once, at
    its next boot: Debian's files copied to the root file system and
    compared before anything is switched, the old fstab and install.conf
    kept (.bak), nothing on the boot partition touched. Our tools find the
    boot partition through BOOT_ROOT. Gate: test/boot-layout-test.sh
    (mutants verify, live, bootroot, marker).
  * Restart to finish updating: sg-restart-notify (the session's helper
    loop) shows sg-shell's sg-restart-notice once a boot while
    /run/reboot-required is there; the kernel-install plugin
    93-sg-reboot-required and sg-kernel-entries write it for a kernel newer
    than the running one (the Surface install already did). Gate:
    test/restart-notify-test.sh (mutant: no memory of the boot).

