sg-session (0.1.0-90) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Sound on laptops with Intel's SOF audio DSP: the initrd started the
    driver before /usr/lib/firmware was mounted, the firmware failed to load
    and the machine had only "Dummy Output" (Latitude 3520). The new
    sg-firmware-retry.service binds such a device (no sound card) again once
    the root file system is up. rtkit for PipeWire's realtime priority.
  * The clock is set from the network: the initrd's systemd-networkd left
    /run/systemd/netif/state saying "off", so timesyncd never synced, a clock
    hours behind made every apt signature "not live until" later and apt
    update failed with signing errors. A timesyncd drop-in removes the stale
    file unless networkd runs. Test: test/firmware-retry-test.sh (make
    test-firmware-retry; its --mutant fails).
  * Firmware for the early drivers: the initrd carries only the firmware
    modules name, and many make the name up as they run (SOF sound,
    Bluetooth, i915's power management) -- they failed from the initrd and
    never asked again. sg-firmware-initrd (sg-firmware-initrd.service at
    boot, a kernel-install plugin for new kernels) keeps the files the
    kernel failed to load that /usr/lib/firmware has, and writes them as
    sg-firmware.initrd into each installed boot entry. Test:
    test/firmware-initrd-test.sh (a Latitude 3520's kernel log; --mutant).
  * Third-party drivers (Setup's checkbox) are kept current: the choice
    stays (drivers.auto) and every boot installs what the hardware asks
    for -- by device as before, and now the firmware the kernel asked for
    and did not find (isenkram's firmware-to-package tables). A package the
    archive does not have is skipped instead of failing the rest (the
    Latitude's first boot stopped there and installed nothing); after new
    firmware the firmware initrd is made again and the devices that failed
    (not the display) are bound again. test/drivers-test.sh covers the
    install path (--mutant: the firmware lookup left out).

